# An Interactive Journey Through Hypergraph Ramsey Avoidance

Pairs are only the beginning. In a 3-uniform hypergraph, every triple receives a color, and a five-set is monochromatic only when all ten of its triples agree. Begin by manipulating the subset count directly.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

The key transformation promotes each colored $r$-subset to a vertex of a new constraint hypergraph. Every candidate $k$-set becomes an edge containing its $ inom{k}{r}$ internal subsets.

{{algorithm:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the exact equivalence</summary>

A proper red-blue coloring of the incidence vertices gives both colors inside every constraint edge, hence no monochromatic candidate set. Conversely, any avoiding Ramsey coloring properly colors every incidence edge. Thus Ramsey avoidance and Property B are equivalent.

</details>

## The first-moment threshold

A fixed edge of size $m$ is monochromatic with probability $2^{1-m}$. If there are fewer than $2^{m-1}$ edges, the expected number of bad edges is below one, so a zero-bad-edge coloring exists.

