## Computation without overclaiming

Sampling illustrates the expected count, while backtracking can exhibit a concrete coloring. Neither a few samples nor several small exact cases establish an asymptotic law.

{{algorithm:2}}

{{demo:2}}

{{visualization:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal complexity and limitations</summary>

There are $ inom{n}{r}$ binary variables, so naive exhaustive search has $2^{ inom{n}{r}}$ assignments. Checking one assignment naively costs $O( inom{n}{k} inom{k}{r})$. The proved criterion gives a single-exponential lower-bound scale for triples; the conjectured double-exponential growth remains open.

</details>

For background, see introductory resources on [Ramsey theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ramsey%27s_theorem), [hypergraphs](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hypergraph), and the [probabilistic method](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Probabilistic_method).
